    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola",
    "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}=1.$",
    "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 9-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-6,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,-6)$ and $(0,2).$",
